Found during k128. After a crash the editor is restarted automatically after a delay, and that automatic start keeps Saved/Crashes and skips the crash-reporter kill. A manual ue_start (or console start) issued during that delay replaces the automatic restart, but it is treated as a manual start: with unreal.cleanOnRestart it empties Saved/Crashes, and the dumps of the crash that just happened are lost.

Done when: a start that replaces a pending post-crash restart keeps the crash evidence the same way the automatic restart would, with a test.

Fix (worktree, uncommitted): the supervisor already tracks the pending restart (backoff + backoffAuto in runService). StartService/RestartService now check it before clearing the backoff and pass Start.ReplacesRestart to PreStart; followsCrash (cmd/launcher/lifecycle.go) counts it, so that start keeps Saved/Crashes and skips the crash-reporter kill exactly like the automatic restart. Covers the policy's retry of a failed start too; the delayed first start is not a pending restart. Log reason: 'Recovery: Saved/Crashes kept (start in place of the pending automatic restart — ...)'.
Design choice (mine): a new Start field instead of reporting the start as Automatic — Automatic is documented and tested as 'the restart policy started it', and the kept-log line would have said 'automatic restart' for a ue_start.
Tests: TestRunCleanOnRestartKeepsCrashesOnAStartDuringTheRestartDelay (run-level, ue_start over MCP during a 600 s delay; red before the fix: 'Saved/Crashes emptied'), TestPreStartSaysWhetherTheStartReplacesAPendingRestart (supervisor: StartService, RestartService, failed-start retry, start delay), new ReplacesRestart row in TestEditorPreStartSparesTheCrashReporterAfterACrash. Mutating replacesRestart() or followsCrash turns each red.
